  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is Flexible Dieting (IIFYM)?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Flexible dieting, commonly called IIFYM ("If It Fits Your Macros"), is an approach to eating built
        around hitting daily targets for protein, carbohydrate, and fat grams — and usually a total calorie
        target — rather than following a fixed list of allowed or forbidden foods. As long as a food or meal
        fits within those macro and calorie targets, it's permitted; nothing is categorically off-limits. It
        grew out of bodybuilding and physique communities as an alternative to rigid, all-or-nothing "clean
        eating" meal plans. It is distinct from the flexitarian diet, a separate, plant-forward eating pattern
        covered elsewhere on this site — flexible dieting is about macro targets, not food-group choices.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        IIFYM stands for <strong>"If It Fits Your Macros."</strong> No foods are categorically banned — the
        only hard requirement is hitting daily protein, carbohydrate, and fat gram targets (and usually total
        calories); what you actually eat to get there is open. It emerged from bodybuilding and physique
        communities in the 2010s as a reaction against rigid "clean eating" meal plans, popularized by online
        coaches and forums rather than originating as an academic or clinical protocol.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Worth being direct about upfront: no peer-reviewed study has evaluated "IIFYM" by that name — it's a
        bodybuilding-forum term, not an academic construct. What the literature actually studies is a related
        but distinct concept from clinical/behavioral nutrition research: <strong>"flexible" vs. "rigid"
        dietary restraint</strong>, studied since the 1990s. Flexible restraint (adaptable, no all-or-nothing
        rules) is associated with better long-term adherence and weight maintenance than rigid restraint — but
        most of that supporting evidence is correlational, not causal, and it's not identical to modern IIFYM
        macro-tracking.
      </p>
      <strong>RCT using macro-based tracking:</strong> Conlin LA, Aguilar DT, Rogers GE, Campbell BI.
      "Flexible vs. rigid dieting in resistance-trained individuals seeking to optimize their physiques: A
      randomized controlled trial," <em>Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition</em>, 2021. A
      20-week trial (23 resistance-trained adults: 10 weeks of active dieting in a ~20% deficit, followed by 10
      weeks of ad-libitum eating) found flexible and rigid dieting produced similar fat loss during active
      dieting, but 91% of the flexible group gained fat-free mass afterward versus only 25% of the rigid group.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://jissn.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12970-021-00452-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Foundational correlational study:</strong> Westenhoefer J, Stunkard AJ, Pudel V. "Validation of
      the flexible and rigid control dimensions of dietary restraint," <em>International Journal of Eating
      Disorders</em>, 1999. Rigid control was associated with higher BMI, more disinhibited eating, and more
      frequent binge episodes; flexible control showed the opposite pattern plus greater weight-loss success —
      correlational, not causal.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10349584/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Additional research:</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Smith CF, Williamson DA, Bray GA, Ryan DH. "Flexible vs. Rigid Dieting Strategies: Relationship with
      Adverse Behavioral Outcomes," <em>Appetite</em>, 1999. In 223 adults, rigid (not flexible) dieting
      correlated with more eating-disorder symptoms, mood disturbance, and body-image preoccupation.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10336790/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
      <br /><br />
      Westenhoefer J, Engel D, Holst C, et al. "Cognitive and weight-related correlates of flexible and rigid
      restrained eating behaviour," <em>Eating Behaviors</em>, 2013. In a large European cohort, flexible
      restraint predicted greater weight loss and better maintenance in regression models; rigid restraint
      predicted worse outcomes on both.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23265405/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Food Examples</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        A scoop of ice cream (roughly 15g fat, 24g carb) can replace part of a day's remaining carb and fat
        allowance; a slice of pizza can substitute for a meal's protein-carb-fat combination — as long as the
        day's running totals still land on target. The caveat every legitimate IIFYM guide flags: hitting
        macro numbers says nothing about fiber, vitamins, minerals, or protein quality. A day built mostly
        around processed, low-fiber choices can meet its macro targets while still being micronutrient-poor —
        the flexibility is real, but it's not a free pass to ignore food quality entirely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">A Sample Day</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Breakfast:</strong> Greek yogurt with berries and granola.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Lunch:</strong> chicken breast, rice, mixed vegetables, olive oil.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Snack:</strong> a protein shake with a banana.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Dinner:</strong> two slices of pizza with a side salad, portions adjusted to close out the
        day's remaining protein, carb, fat, and calorie totals.
        <br /><br />
        This is illustrative, not a target — your actual gram targets come from your calorie goal and chosen
        macro split.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who It Might Suit</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Tends to suit people who do well with structure-light, numbers-based tracking and want the social and
        travel flexibility of not having off-limits foods. It may not suit people who consistently use that
        flexibility to choose low-nutrient-density foods, or — per the restraint research above — people who
        do better psychologically with concrete food rules; rigid restraint isn't shown to be worse for
        everyone, just worse on average across these study populations. It also requires consistent tracking
        to actually work, which isn't the right fit for everyone's relationship with food.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

